Kubernetes Upgrade on EKS / AKS / GKE (Super Easy Guide) 🚀

Upgrading Kubernetes on managed services is much easier because cloud providers handle most of the complexity.

☁️ EKS (AWS Elastic Kubernetes Service)

🔹 Upgrade Control Plane

aws eks update-cluster-version \
--name <cluster-name> \
--kubernetes-version <new-version>

🔹 Upgrade Worker Nodes

Create a new node group with the new version

Or upgrade managed node groups

Old nodes are drained automatically

 Minimal effort, zero downtime if configured properly.


☁️ AKS (Azure Kubernetes Service)

🔹 Check available versions

az aks get-upgrades --name <cluster-name> --resource-group <rg>


🔹 Upgrade cluster

az aks upgrade \
--name <cluster-name> \
--resource-group <rg> \
--kubernetes-version <new-version>
🔹 Node pools are upgraded one by one

 Azure handles pod draining automatically.


☁️ GKE (Google Kubernetes Engine)
🔹 Upgrade Control Plane

bash
Copy code
gcloud container clusters upgrade <cluster-name> \
--master --cluster-version <new-version>
🔹 Upgrade Node Pools

gcloud container node-pools upgrade <node-pool-name> \
--cluster <cluster-name>

 Very smooth upgrades with strong automation.

💡 Best Practices for Managed K8s

 Upgrade control plane first
 Upgrade node pools gradually
 Enable auto-upgrade in non-prod
 Monitor pods & workloads

Managed Kubernetes makes upgrades safe, fast, and production-ready.
